          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life</strong><br>\nEmily Brontë was born in 1818 in Thornton, Yorkshire, England, as the fifth of six children. Her father was a clergyman, and her mother died when she was just three years old. She and her siblings spent most of their early life in the rural parsonage of Haworth, which provided a solitary and scenic backdrop that influenced her writing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and Creativity</strong><br>\nThe Brontë children were educated at home by their father and aunt. They created complex fantasy worlds and wrote stories about them, which helped to develop their creative writing skills. Emily was particularly close to her sister Anne and her brother Branwell during these early years.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Life at Haworth Parsonage</strong><br>\nAfter attending a brief period of school, Emily returned home to Haworth Parsonage, where she lived for most of her life. She performed household duties and enjoyed the Yorkshire moors, which inspired much of her poetry and her novel.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
